### Sigma Protocols and Fiat-Shamir
*Slide Deck:* [Sigma protocols and Fiat-Shamir](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-cfrg-sessb-sigma-protocols-and-fiat-shamir-04)
* *Presenter:* Michele Orrù

* **Presentation Highlights:**
  * Michele Orrù presented an update on the two specifications: Sigma protocols and the Fiat-Shamir transformation.
  * Scope clarification: Sigma protocols keep compositions and compressed proofs out of scope to maintain simplicity. The Fiat-Shamir specification supports one-round protocols (Sigma proofs) as well as multi-round interactive oracle proofs (IOPs).
  * **Formal Verification:** In collaboration with Arqlib, a Lean formalization of the Fiat-Shamir draft was created, proving soundness/knowledge soundness, straight-line extractability, and zero-knowledge in the Random Oracle Model (ROM).
  * **Security Analysis & Malleability:** Highlighted common implementation pitfalls in weak Fiat-Shamir transformations (e.g., LibSignal issues, point encoding malleability, non-prefix-free serializations) and provided adversarial test vectors.

* **Discussion:**
  * Christopher Patton asked if any issues affect `draft-ietf-cfrg-vdaf`. Michele Orrù indicated he would review the spec.
  * Watson Ladd noted that the draft currently lacks an easy way to hook into serialization for OR compositions (e.g., in `draft-ladd-cfrg-ec-vrf` / ECI hat). Michele explained that codecs are modular and offered to coordinate offline on prefix-free encoding requirements.
  * Shai Levin asked in chat if cut-and-choose techniques were considered for the Sigma standard. Michele clarified that while not in the Sigma protocol draft itself, the Fiat-Shamir transformation can support tree structures.

