### Longfellow ZK
*Slide Deck:* [Longfellow ZK](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-cfrg-sessb-longfellow-zk-00)
* *Presenter:* Abhi Shelat

* **Presentation Highlights:**
  * Abhi Shelat presented updates on `draft-ietf-cfrg-longfellow-zk`, a transparent hash-based zero-knowledge argument system used to prove statements about legacy algorithms (ECDSA and SHA) for digital identity protocols.
  * **Formal Verification:** Security properties (knowledge soundness and zero-knowledge) have been formally proven in Lean.
  * **Performance & Rust Rewrite:** Transitioned to Rust; the v2 implementation demonstrates ~4x faster prover time (~65 ms), ~3x smaller proofs (~136 KB), 40 MB memory usage, and 127 bits of soundness in GF($2^{128}$). Verified MLDSA-44 and MLDSA-65 benchmarks run in ~36–37 ms.
  * **Proposed Draft Updates (-01 / -02):**
    1. *Fiat-Shamir Query Structure:* Replaced classic transcript hashing with a directed graph structure for transcript nodes. This tightens the security reduction (eliminating union bound degradation to achieve 127-bit soundness) and enables parallelized multi-threaded provers (requested by Apple/Kathy).
    2. *Circuit Representation:* Introduced grammar-based circuit compression to significantly reduce memory overhead without needing full circuit expansion in memory.

* **Discussion:**
  * Watson Ladd asked about straight-line extractability and whether avoiding rewinding carries a performance penalty. Abhi explained that Reed-Solomon encoding redundancy on the witness enables straight-line extraction without a forking lemma penalty.
  * Michele Orrù suggested integrating the graph-based challenge derivation concept into the main Fiat-Shamir draft. Abhi agreed to submit a write-up alongside co-author Kathy.
  * Michele Orrù asked if benchmark numbers were single-threaded. Abhi confirmed all reported benchmark numbers are single-core/single-threaded to match execution constraints on mobile platforms (e.g., Android).
  * Christopher Patton and David Waite confirmed the open-source Rust implementation is available on GitHub under the Linux Foundation / Post-Quantum Cryptography Alliance (PQCA).

