### Schnorr-Type Proofs of Possession for ECDSA Device Binding
*Slide Deck:* [Schnorr-Type Proofs of Possession for ECDSA Device Binding](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-cfrg-sessb-schnorr-type-proofs-of-possession-for-ecdsa-device-binding-00)
* *Presenters:* Alexandros Zacharakis, Shai Levin

* **Presentation Highlights:**
  * Alexandros Zacharakis and Shai Levin presented a proposal for ECDSA device binding in privacy-preserving credentials (such as EU Digital Identity / EUDI Wallets) using existing hardware secure elements.
  * Zero-knowledge proofs are used to demonstrate possession of an issuer-signed credential containing a hidden device public key and a signature on a verifier-supplied nonce under that key.
  * **Construction:** Leverages the committee disclosure framework. Converts BLS commitments from BBS signatures to the Too (T256) curve, emulating P-256 scalar field arithmetic in T256 using Schnorr-type Sigma protocols without trusted setups or circuits.
  * **Performance:** Execution takes <70 ms on newer devices and <300 ms on older hardware using multithreading, with proof sizes <100 KB.
  * The draft aligns with the CFRG Sigma protocol specification for linear relations and requests Working Group adoption.

* **Discussion:**
  * Rowan asked whether the underlying technique would apply to Edwards curves. Shai Levin responded that while curve arithmetic differs, the conceptual framework should apply without fundamental obstructions.

